NEW=[
("shared",None,S("sc-found","NLP Foundations: Word Vectors to Scaling Laws",
"Knowing how we got from word2vec to frontier models makes today's design choices (context, scale, data, interpretability) make sense.",
"Modern LLMs are the end of a short chain of ideas. Word embeddings (word2vec, GloVe) showed that meaning can be captured as vectors learned from co-occurrence. Recurrent networks (RNNs, LSTMs) processed sequences token by token but struggled with long-range dependencies and could not parallelize across a sequence. Attention, then the transformer, removed recurrence and let every token look at every other token in parallel, which made training on vast data practical.\n\nPretraining on next-token prediction over trillions of tokens produces general capabilities; scaling laws (Kaplan et al., then Chinchilla) describe how loss falls predictably with parameters, data and compute, and that compute-optimal training uses roughly 20 tokens per parameter. Data curation (deduplication, quality filtering, mixtures) matters as much as size. Interpretability research (probing, attention analysis, sparse autoencoders) tries to explain what the network learned.\n\nCommon mistakes: assuming bigger is always better instead of compute-optimal; ignoring data quality; treating attention maps as full explanations.\n\nMental model: every generation fixed the previous one's bottleneck: meaning, then sequence, then parallelism, then scale.",
[R("Stanford CS224N","https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs224n/"),R("Stanford CS336: Language Modeling from Scratch","https://stanford-cs336.github.io/spring2025/"),R("Chinchilla: Training compute-optimal LLMs (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556"),R("Anthropic: Towards monosemanticity (sparse autoencoders)","https://transformer-circuits.pub/2023/monosemantic-features")],
"Train word2vec on a small corpus and test analogies; train a tiny LSTM and a tiny transformer on the same character-level data and compare loss and speed; then fit a scaling-law curve from 4 model sizes and predict the loss of a 5th.",
[T("sc-found-t1","Word embeddings and recurrent networks","Word2vec learns a vector per word by predicting neighbors (skip-gram) or the center word (CBOW); similar words end up close, and some relations become vector offsets. Embeddings are static: one vector per word regardless of context. RNNs and LSTMs added context by reading tokens sequentially with a hidden state; LSTM gates reduced vanishing gradients, but sequential processing limited training speed and long-range memory. These limits motivated attention, first added to RNN encoders for translation, and then the attention-only transformer.",["Skip-gram and CBOW","Static vs contextual embeddings","RNN hidden state","LSTM gates and vanishing gradients","Seq2seq with attention"]),
T("sc-found-t2","Pretraining and scaling laws","Pretraining minimizes next-token prediction loss on web, code, books and curated data. Scaling laws show loss decreasing as a power law in parameters N, tokens D and compute C, roughly C ≈ 6ND FLOPs. Chinchilla found earlier models were undertrained and that compute-optimal training uses about 20 tokens per parameter, though production models are often trained far beyond that to make inference cheaper. Data pipelines with deduplication, quality filters and domain mixtures often move results more than architecture tweaks.",["Next-token objective","C ≈ 6ND compute rule","Compute-optimal vs overtrained models","Data deduplication and filtering","Data mixtures"]),
T("sc-found-t3","Interpretability basics","Interpretability asks what representations and circuits a model uses. Probing trains a small classifier on hidden states to test whether a property is encoded. Attention visualization shows where heads look but not why. Mechanistic interpretability identifies circuits, and sparse autoencoders decompose activations into many more interpretable features, some of which can be steered. For engineers, interpretability is mostly a research lens today, but it informs safety evaluations and debugging of fine-tuned behavior.",["Linear probes","Attention visualization limits","Circuits","Sparse autoencoders and features","Steering and safety uses"])],
[Q("Why did transformers replace RNNs for large-scale training?",["Fewer parameters","They process all tokens in parallel and handle long-range dependencies better","They need no data","They use no attention"],1,"Removing recurrence enables parallel training and direct long-range attention."),
Q("Chinchilla's compute-optimal rule of thumb is about…",["1 token per parameter","20 tokens per parameter","1,000 tokens per parameter","No relation"],1,"Chinchilla suggests roughly 20 training tokens per parameter for compute-optimal training."),
Q("A 7B model trained on 2T tokens needs roughly how many training FLOPs (C ≈ 6ND)?",["8.4 × 10^22","8.4 × 10^19","8.4 × 10^25","8.4 × 10^16"],0,"6 × 7e9 × 2e12 = 8.4e22 FLOPs.")],
["attention"])),
("agentic","g-l1",S("g-patterns","Agentic Design Patterns: Reflection, Planning, Tools, Multi-Agent, Deep Research",
"A handful of reusable patterns explain almost every agent architecture; knowing them lets you pick the simplest one that works.",
"Andrew Ng's four agentic design patterns are a useful vocabulary. Reflection: the model critiques and revises its own output (or a second model does). Tool use: the model calls functions, search, code or APIs to act and to get facts. Planning: the model decomposes a goal into steps (ReAct interleaves reasoning and acting; plan-and-execute writes a plan, then runs it). Multi-agent: specialized agents collaborate through an orchestrator or hand-offs.\n\nAnthropic's 'Building effective agents' adds workflow patterns that are often better than free agents: prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers and evaluator-optimizer. The broader idea is augmented language models: an LLM plus retrieval, tools and memory.\n\nDeep research agents combine these: plan sub-questions, run many searches in parallel (often with built-in web search, file search and code interpreter tools), read and extract evidence, reflect on gaps, and write a cited report.\n\nCommon mistakes: starting with multi-agent when one agent with good tools would do; reflection loops with no stopping rule; plans that are never re-checked against observations.\n\nMental model: use workflows when the steps are known, agents when they are not, and add autonomy only where it pays.",
[R("Anthropic: Building effective agents","https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"),R("DeepLearning.AI: Agentic AI (Andrew Ng)","https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/agentic-ai/"),R("ReAct paper (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629"),R("Anthropic: How we built our multi-agent research system","https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system")],
"Implement the same research task four ways: single prompt, prompt chain, ReAct agent with web search, and orchestrator plus parallel research sub-agents with a reflection step. Compare citation accuracy, coverage, cost and latency on 15 questions.",
[T("g-patterns-t1","Reflection and evaluator-optimizer","Reflection asks the model to critique its draft against criteria, then revise. It helps most when feedback is concrete: failing tests, schema errors, a checklist, or a judge with a rubric. The evaluator-optimizer workflow makes this explicit with a separate evaluator. Always cap iterations and stop when the evaluator passes or improvement stalls. Reflection without external signal can entrench errors, so prefer tool-grounded feedback such as running the code or checking the citation.",["Self-critique against criteria","External feedback (tests, validators)","Evaluator-optimizer loops","Iteration caps","When reflection hurts"]),
T("g-patterns-t2","Planning, routing and workflows","ReAct interleaves a thought, an action and an observation each step, which adapts well but can wander. Plan-and-execute writes a full plan first, then executes and re-plans on failure, which is cheaper and easier to audit. Routing classifies the input and sends it to a specialized prompt or model. Prompt chaining and parallelization handle known multi-step tasks deterministically. Pick the least autonomous pattern that meets the requirement, and log plans so failures are debuggable.",["Augmented language models: retrieval, tools, memory","ReAct loop","Plan-and-execute with re-planning","Routing to specialists","Prompt chaining","Parallelization and voting"]),
T("g-patterns-t3","Multi-agent systems and deep research","Multi-agent designs split work across specialized agents: an orchestrator that delegates to workers, or peer hand-offs. They help with parallel breadth such as researching ten sub-topics at once, and with separation of privileges. They cost more tokens and add coordination failures. Deep research agents plan sub-questions, fan out parallel searches with built-in web and file search tools, extract evidence with sources, reflect on gaps, and synthesize a cited report. Evaluate citation accuracy, not just fluency.",["Orchestrator-worker","Hand-offs between agents","Parallel research fan-out","Evidence extraction with citations","Token cost of multi-agent"])],
[Q("Which pattern fits a task whose steps are fully known in advance?",["Free-form autonomous agent","Prompt chaining / workflow","Multi-agent debate","Reflection only"],1,"Workflows are cheaper and more reliable when steps are known."),
Q("Reflection works best when…",["The model critiques with no external signal","Feedback comes from tests, validators or a rubric-based judge","Iterations are unlimited","Temperature is high"],1,"Concrete external signal makes revisions converge."),
Q("Main benefit of multi-agent deep research?",["Lower cost","Parallel breadth across many sub-questions","No need for citations","Fewer tool calls"],1,"Parallel sub-agents cover more ground at higher token cost.")],
["agent-loop"])),
("agentic","g-l2",S("g-frameworks","Agent Frameworks and Managed Agent Platforms",
"Teams lose months picking frameworks; knowing what each one actually provides lets you choose in a day and stay portable.",
"Agent frameworks package the loop, tools, state, memory, tracing and multi-agent coordination. Open frameworks: LangGraph (explicit state graphs, checkpointing, human-in-the-loop interrupts, streaming; traced with LangSmith), OpenAI Agents SDK (agents, tools, hand-offs, guardrails, tracing), Claude Agent SDK (the harness behind Claude Code: tools, permissions, sub-agents, context compaction), Google ADK (Agent Development Kit, multi-agent, A2A support), CrewAI and AutoGen (role-based and conversational multi-agent), Hugging Face smolagents (minimal code agents), and LlamaIndex (workflows and query engines over data).\n\nManaged platforms run agents for you with enterprise identity, logging and scaling: AWS Bedrock AgentCore (runtime, memory, identity, gateway, observability) alongside Bedrock Agents and Knowledge Bases; Google Vertex AI Agent Engine; Azure AI Foundry Agent Service and Copilot Studio; Databricks Mosaic AI Agent Framework with Agent Evaluation, Genie and Unity Catalog; Snowflake Cortex Agents.\n\nCommon mistakes: choosing by GitHub stars; deep framework lock-in for logic that is 200 lines of plain code; ignoring how the framework handles state persistence, tracing and identity.\n\nMental model: frameworks are harness kits. Keep your tools (MCP), evals and data framework-agnostic so you can swap the harness.",
[R("LangGraph docs","https://langchain-ai.github.io/langgraph/"),R("OpenAI Agents SDK","https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/"),R("Claude Agent SDK overview","https://docs.claude.com/en/api/agent-sdk/overview"),R("Google Agent Development Kit (ADK)","https://google.github.io/adk-docs/"),R("Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/agentcore/"),R("Hugging Face smolagents","https://huggingface.co/docs/smolagents/index")],
"Build one support agent (two tools, one MCP server, human approval for refunds) in three stacks: plain Python, LangGraph, and one of OpenAI Agents SDK / Claude Agent SDK / Google ADK. Deploy one version on a managed platform (Bedrock AgentCore, Vertex AI Agent Engine or Azure AI Foundry Agent Service). Compare lines of code, tracing, state persistence, identity integration and eval results.",
[T("g-frameworks-t1","Open-source agent frameworks","LangGraph models agents as state graphs with nodes and edges, persistent checkpoints, interrupts for human approval and streaming, and pairs with LangSmith for tracing and evals. The OpenAI Agents SDK is a light layer with agents, tools, hand-offs, guardrails and built-in tracing. The Claude Agent SDK exposes the Claude Code harness: file and shell tools, permissions, sub-agents and context compaction. Google ADK supports multi-agent hierarchies and A2A. CrewAI and AutoGen focus on role-based or conversational multi-agent setups, smolagents on minimal code-writing agents, and LlamaIndex on data-centric workflows and query engines.",["LangGraph graphs, checkpoints, interrupts","OpenAI Agents SDK hand-offs and guardrails","Claude Agent SDK and Claude Code harness","Google ADK and A2A","CrewAI, AutoGen, smolagents, LlamaIndex"]),
T("g-frameworks-t2","Managed agent platforms","Managed platforms trade flexibility for enterprise plumbing. Bedrock AgentCore provides a serverless agent runtime, memory, identity, a tool gateway and observability for agents built with any framework; Bedrock Agents and Knowledge Bases offer configured agents and managed RAG. Vertex AI Agent Engine hosts ADK or LangGraph agents with sessions and memory. Azure AI Foundry Agent Service and Copilot Studio integrate with Entra identity and Microsoft 365. Databricks Mosaic AI Agent Framework adds Agent Evaluation, Genie text-to-SQL and Unity Catalog governance; Snowflake offers Cortex Agents.",["Bedrock AgentCore, Agents, Knowledge Bases","Vertex AI Agent Engine","Azure AI Foundry Agent Service, Copilot Studio","Databricks Mosaic AI and Agent Evaluation","Snowflake Cortex Agents"]),
T("g-frameworks-t3","Choosing and staying portable","Choose on: state and durability model, human-in-the-loop support, tracing and eval integration, identity and secrets handling, multi-agent needs, language support, and where it will run. Keep the valuable parts portable: tools as MCP servers, prompts in a registry, evals in your own harness, and data behind your own APIs. Wrap framework calls behind a thin interface. Re-evaluate yearly; the landscape moves fast, and a plain loop with good tools often beats a heavy framework.",["Selection criteria","Tools via MCP for portability","Framework-agnostic evals","Thin abstraction layers","Build vs adopt"])],
[Q("Which LangGraph feature supports human approval mid-run?",["Embeddings","Interrupts with persisted checkpoints","Tokenizers","Quantization"],1,"Interrupts pause the graph and checkpoints let it resume after approval."),
Q("What is the best way to stay portable across agent frameworks?",["Write all logic inside one framework's classes","Expose tools as MCP servers and keep evals and data framework-agnostic","Avoid tracing","Use only managed platforms"],1,"Portable tools, evals and data make the harness swappable."),
Q("Which managed service offers an agent runtime, memory, identity and tool gateway on AWS?",["Amazon S3","Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","AWS Glue","Amazon SQS"],1,"Bedrock AgentCore provides those agent runtime services.")],
["agent-loop"])),
("engineer","e-l2",S("e-reasoning","Reasoning Models, Test-Time Compute and Model API Platform Features",
"Reasoning models and platform features (stateful APIs, caching, batch, built-in tools) change the cost and quality math of every agent.",
"Reasoning models (OpenAI o-series and GPT reasoning modes, Claude with extended thinking, Gemini thinking, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen reasoning) spend extra tokens thinking before answering. This test-time compute boosts math, coding, planning and multi-step tool use, controlled by settings such as reasoning effort or a thinking budget. The trade-off is latency and cost: thinking tokens are billed, and simple tasks gain nothing.\n\nModel APIs now bundle platform features that agents should use deliberately: the OpenAI Responses API with stored conversation state and built-in tools (web search, file search, code interpreter, computer use); prompt caching across OpenAI, Anthropic and Gemini; batch APIs at roughly half price for offline jobs; structured outputs; citations; moderation endpoints; and fine-tuning including reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) with graders. Cloud platforms mirror these: Bedrock batch inference, model evaluation and Guardrails; Azure AI Foundry evaluations and content safety.\n\nCommon mistakes: max reasoning effort everywhere; dropping prior reasoning items between tool calls so the model re-thinks; online calls for work that could be batched; no caching on long stable system prompts.\n\nMental model: reasoning effort is a dial on cost per correct answer. Measure it.",
[R("OpenAI reasoning models guide","https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/reasoning"),R("Anthropic extended thinking","https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/extended-thinking"),R("OpenAI Batch API","https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/batch"),R("OpenAI reinforcement fine-tuning","https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/reinforcement-fine-tuning"),R("Amazon Bedrock model evaluation","https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/evaluation.html")],
"On 50 multi-step tasks, compare a non-reasoning model with a reasoning model at low, medium and high effort. Report accuracy, latency, total tokens and cost per correct answer. Then move an offline summarization job to a batch API and add prompt caching to the agent's system prompt, and measure the savings.",
[T("e-reasoning-t1","Reasoning models and test-time compute","Reasoning models are trained, typically with reinforcement learning on verifiable tasks, to produce long internal chains of thought before answering. More thinking tokens generally improve accuracy on hard problems, with diminishing returns. APIs expose this as reasoning effort levels or thinking token budgets. For agents, reasoning helps planning and tool selection; keep reasoning state across tool calls where the API supports it (for example, passing back reasoning items or thinking blocks) to avoid repeated thinking. Route easy requests to non-reasoning models.",["How reasoning models are trained","Reasoning effort and thinking budgets","Interleaved thinking with tools","Preserving reasoning state","Routing easy vs hard requests"]),
T("e-reasoning-t2","Stateful APIs, built-in tools, caching and batch","The Responses API can store conversation state server-side and run built-in tools such as web search, file search over uploaded files, code interpreter and computer use, which removes boilerplate but couples you to a vendor. Prompt caching discounts repeated prefixes, so put stable instructions and tool definitions first. Batch APIs process large offline jobs asynchronously at about half price within a 24-hour window; Bedrock batch inference is the cloud equivalent. Use streaming for user-facing latency and batch for everything that can wait.",["Responses API and stored state","Built-in web search, file search, code interpreter","Prompt caching and prefix ordering","Batch APIs and Bedrock batch inference","Streaming vs batch"]),
T("e-reasoning-t3","Vendor fine-tuning, evaluation and safety services","Hosted fine-tuning covers supervised fine-tuning, preference tuning and reinforcement fine-tuning, where you supply tasks and graders and the vendor runs RL on a reasoning model. Managed evaluation services (Bedrock model evaluation, Azure AI Foundry evaluations, OpenAI Evals) run LLM-as-judge and human evaluation jobs. Moderation and content safety APIs (OpenAI Moderation, Azure AI Content Safety with Prompt Shields, Bedrock Guardrails) classify harmful content and injection attempts. Use them as building blocks, while keeping your own golden sets as the source of truth.",["SFT, preference tuning, RFT","Graders for RFT","Managed evaluation jobs: Bedrock model evaluation, Azure AI Foundry","Moderation and content safety APIs","Keeping your own golden sets"])],
[Q("A model at high reasoning effort is 2% more accurate but 4x the cost on simple FAQ questions. Best action?",["Use high effort everywhere","Route simple questions to low effort or a non-reasoning model","Disable caching","Use batch for chat"],1,"Optimize cost per correct answer by routing by difficulty."),
Q("Where should stable instructions and tool definitions go to benefit from prompt caching?",["At the end of the prompt","At the start, as a stable prefix","In random order","In the user message"],1,"Caches match on identical prefixes."),
Q("Which workload fits a batch API?",["Live voice agent","Nightly classification of 200k documents","Interactive chat","Real-time fraud alert"],1,"Batch suits large, latency-tolerant offline jobs at lower price.")],
["model-router"])),
("engineer","e-l5",S("e-rlenv","RL Environments, Reward Design and Prompt Optimization",
"Post-training for agents is now mostly environment and reward engineering; the quality of the environment decides what the model learns.",
"Reinforcement learning for LLM agents needs three things: an environment (tasks plus sandboxed tools such as code execution, a browser or a simulated company), a reward (verifiable checks like tests passing or a correct database state, or graders and reward models), and an RL algorithm (PPO, GRPO and variants) run by a framework such as verl, OpenRLHF or TRL with fast rollouts on vLLM.\n\nEnvironment design is the hard part: tasks must be diverse, realistic, solvable and verifiable, at the right difficulty for the current model. Reward hacking is the central failure: the policy finds ways to score without solving the task, such as editing tests, special-casing graders or exploiting simulator bugs. Defenses include hidden tests, multiple independent graders, monitoring trajectories and held-out evals.\n\nBefore spending GPUs on RL, prompt and program optimization can capture much of the gain: DSPy compiles prompts and few-shot examples against a metric, and GEPA evolves prompts using reflective feedback on trajectories. Model merging (for example with mergekit) combines fine-tuned checkpoints without further training.\n\nCommon mistakes: rewards that can be gamed; environments far easier than production; no held-out evaluation; training on contaminated benchmark data.\n\nMental model: the model optimizes exactly what you reward, in the world you built for it.",
[R("verl (Volcano Engine RL for LLMs)","https://github.com/volcengine/verl"),R("DeepSeekMath (GRPO) paper (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300"),R("DSPy","https://dspy.ai/"),R("GEPA: Reflective prompt evolution (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19457"),R("mergekit","https://github.com/arcee-ai/mergekit")],
"Build a sandboxed tool-use environment with 200 verifiable tasks. First optimize the prompt with DSPy or GEPA and record the gain. Then run GRPO on a small open model with verl or TRL, add one deliberate reward loophole, detect the reward hacking from trajectories, close it, and compare held-out results.",
[T("e-rlenv-t1","Environments and verifiable rewards","An RL environment for agents exposes a task, tools and a final check. Verifiable rewards come from deterministic checks: unit tests, exact answers, database end states, or a compiler. Gym-style interfaces (reset, step, reward) make environments reusable. Sandboxed execution in containers keeps rollouts safe and reproducible at scale. Tune difficulty so the model succeeds sometimes; tasks that are always solved or never solved give no learning signal under group-relative methods like GRPO.",["Gym-style reset/step/reward","Verifiable checks and graders","Sandboxed rollouts at scale","Difficulty calibration","Task diversity and realism"]),
T("e-rlenv-t2","Reward hacking and environment quality","Reward hacking happens when the policy maximizes the reward signal without achieving the intended goal, for example deleting failing tests, hard-coding expected outputs or exploiting a lenient judge. Detect it by sampling and reading trajectories, comparing reward with held-out evaluation, and watching for sudden reward jumps. Prevent it with hidden or regenerated tests, read-only graders, multiple reward sources, penalties for forbidden actions, and environment red-teaming before training starts.",["Common reward hacks","Trajectory auditing","Hidden tests and read-only graders","Reward vs held-out divergence","Environment red-teaming"]),
T("e-rlenv-t3","Prompt optimization, RL frameworks and merging","Prompt and program optimizers search over instructions and examples against your metric: DSPy compiles modular programs; GEPA uses reflection on failed trajectories to evolve prompts and often rivals RL at a fraction of the cost. When weights must change, frameworks such as verl, OpenRLHF and TRL orchestrate rollouts, reward computation and PPO or GRPO updates. Model merging averages or combines fine-tuned weights to blend skills cheaply; always re-evaluate merged models.",["DSPy optimizers","GEPA reflective evolution","verl, OpenRLHF, TRL","Rollout throughput with vLLM","Model merging with mergekit"])],
[Q("Why are tasks the model always solves useless for GRPO?",["They are too long","All samples get the same reward, so group-relative advantages are zero","They use too many tokens","They are not verifiable"],1,"GRPO learns from reward differences within a group; identical rewards give no signal."),
Q("Training reward rises sharply but held-out success is flat. Most likely?",["Better learning","Reward hacking","GPU failure","Tokenizer bug"],1,"Divergence between reward and held-out evals signals the policy is gaming the reward."),
Q("What should you try before spending GPUs on RL?",["Nothing","Prompt/program optimization (DSPy, GEPA) against the same metric","Bigger context","More temperature"],1,"Prompt optimization often captures much of the gain cheaply.")],
["rlhf-dpo-grpo"])),
("engineer","e-l5",S("e-genmedia","Image and Video Generation: Diffusion Models in Products",
"Generative media is part of many agent workflows (marketing, design, product content), and it brings its own cost, safety and IP questions.",
"Diffusion models generate images by learning to reverse a noising process: starting from random noise, a network repeatedly predicts and removes noise, guided by a text prompt. Latent diffusion (Stable Diffusion) runs this in a compressed latent space for speed; diffusion transformers (DiT) replaced the U-Net backbone in many recent models; flow matching is a closely related training objective. Video models extend this across time. Control methods (ControlNet, image-to-image, inpainting) and fine-tuning (LoRA, DreamBooth) shape outputs to a brand or subject.\n\nIn agentic products, an agent might write a brief, call an image or video generation API, check the output with a vision model, and iterate. Production concerns: cost per image or second of video, latency, content safety filters, provenance marking (C2PA, and EU AI Act Article 50 duties to mark synthetic content), likeness and IP rights, and human review for public assets.\n\nCommon mistakes: no safety filter on prompts and outputs; no record of model and prompt for provenance; ignoring licensing of training data and outputs.\n\nMental model: generation is cheap to try and expensive to trust; build the review loop first.",
[R("Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.11239"),R("Hugging Face Diffusers docs","https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers/index"),R("C2PA content provenance","https://c2pa.org/"),R("Scalable Diffusion Models with Transformers (DiT, arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09748")],
"Build an agent that turns a product description into three ad images: it writes prompts, calls a diffusion model (local Diffusers or an API), checks each image with a VLM against a brand checklist, regenerates failures, and attaches C2PA provenance. Report cost per approved image and the rejection rate.",
[T("e-genmedia-t1","How diffusion models work","Training adds Gaussian noise to images over many steps and teaches a network to predict that noise given the noisy image, the step and a text embedding. Generation starts from pure noise and denoises step by step. Latent diffusion compresses images with an autoencoder so denoising runs on small latents. Classifier-free guidance strengthens prompt adherence by mixing conditional and unconditional predictions. Fewer sampling steps via better samplers or distillation cut latency at some quality cost.",["Forward noising and reverse denoising","Latent diffusion and autoencoders","U-Net vs diffusion transformers","Classifier-free guidance","Samplers and step distillation"]),
T("e-genmedia-t2","Control, customization and video","Image-to-image, inpainting and outpainting edit existing images. ControlNet and similar adapters condition on edges, poses or depth for layout control. LoRA and DreamBooth fine-tune a model on a few images of a product, style or person. Video diffusion models add temporal layers or operate on spatio-temporal latents, with cost scaling with resolution and duration. Agents can orchestrate these steps, but each one needs automated checks.",["Inpainting and outpainting","ControlNet conditioning","LoRA and DreamBooth customization","Video diffusion","Agent-orchestrated pipelines"]),
T("e-genmedia-t3","Safety, provenance and cost in production","Filter prompts and outputs for disallowed content, and block likeness of real people without consent. Mark outputs as AI-generated with C2PA manifests or watermarks, which supports EU AI Act Article 50 transparency obligations for synthetic content. Log the model, prompt, seed and reviewer for each published asset. Track cost per approved asset, not per generation, since rejected attempts add up. Clarify licensing and IP terms of the model and outputs before commercial use.",["Prompt and output safety filters","Likeness and consent","C2PA provenance and watermarking","Audit logging of generations","Cost per approved asset"])],
[Q("What does a diffusion model learn during training?",["To classify images","To predict the noise added to an image at a given step","To compress text","To rank documents"],1,"It learns to denoise; generation runs that in reverse from noise."),
Q("Why does latent diffusion run faster?",["It skips the prompt","Denoising happens in a compressed latent space","It uses no GPU","It generates text first"],1,"Smaller latents make each denoising step cheaper."),
Q("Which practice supports transparency duties for synthetic images?",["Removing metadata","C2PA provenance or watermark marking","Higher resolution","More sampling steps"],1,"Provenance marking identifies content as AI-generated.")],
[])),
("trust","t-l3",S("t-agentattacks","Agent Attacks Deep Dive: Goal Hijacking, Tool Tampering, Poisoning and Model Extraction",
"Attacks on agents target their goals, tools, memory and the model itself; each needs a specific defense beyond generic guardrails.",
"Beyond basic prompt injection, agentic systems face a wider attack catalog (OWASP's agentic threats guidance and MITRE ATLAS describe many).\n\nGoal hijacking: injected content redirects the agent's objective, for example a web page that tells a browsing agent to buy something else. Tool parameter tampering: the attacker manipulates tool arguments, such as changing a recipient address or amount, often via injected text the model copies into a call. Tool and MCP supply chain: a malicious or compromised MCP server returns poisoned descriptions or outputs ('tool poisoning') or a trusted tool changes behavior after approval ('rug pull'). Memory and context poisoning: attackers plant false facts in long-term memory or retrieval corpora that persist across sessions. Data poisoning: tampered training or fine-tuning data implants backdoors. Model extraction and inversion: repeated queries reconstruct a model's behavior or leak training data; system prompt extraction leaks proprietary instructions.\n\nDefenses: argument validation and policy checks on every tool call, pinning and reviewing tool descriptions, provenance and write controls for memory, data supply-chain checks, rate limiting and anomaly detection against extraction, and assuming system prompts are public.\n\nMental model: every input channel to an agent, whether a page, a tool description, a memory or a dataset, is an attack surface.",
[R("OWASP GenAI Security Project: Agentic threats","https://genai.owasp.org/initiatives/agentic-security-initiative/"),R("MITRE ATLAS","https://atlas.mitre.org/"),R("Invariant Labs: MCP tool poisoning attacks","https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-security-notification-tool-poisoning-attacks"),R("Stealing machine learning models via prediction APIs (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02943")],
"Red-team a browsing and email agent connected to two MCP servers. Demonstrate goal hijacking via a web page, tool parameter tampering of an email recipient, an MCP tool-description poisoning, and memory poisoning that persists to a new session. Implement defenses (argument policies, pinned tool manifests, memory write review, approvals) and report attack success before and after.",
[T("t-agentattacks-t1","Goal hijacking and tool parameter tampering","Goal hijacking replaces the user's objective with the attacker's through injected instructions in content the agent reads. Tool parameter tampering is quieter: the objective stays, but arguments change, such as a payment to a different account or an email to an external address. Defend by validating arguments against policies and the user's original request (allowed recipients, amount limits, domains), showing previews of consequential calls for approval, and keeping untrusted content from flowing into high-privilege tool arguments without checks.",["Goal hijacking via content","Argument tampering patterns","Policy validation of arguments","Previews and approvals","Taint tracking from untrusted inputs"]),
T("t-agentattacks-t2","Tool, MCP and memory poisoning","Tool poisoning hides malicious instructions in an MCP tool's description or outputs that the model reads but the user never sees. Rug pulls change a tool's behavior after it was approved. Memory poisoning writes false facts or instructions into long-term memory or a retrieval corpus so they influence future sessions. Mitigate by pinning and diffing tool manifests, allowlisting MCP servers, isolating servers by privilege, reviewing or scoring memory writes, storing provenance with every memory, and expiring unverified memories.",["Tool description poisoning","Rug pulls and manifest pinning","MCP server allowlists","Memory poisoning across sessions","Provenance and expiry for memories"]),
T("t-agentattacks-t3","Data poisoning, model extraction and prompt leakage","Data poisoning inserts crafted examples into training or fine-tuning data to create backdoors triggered by specific phrases; vet data sources, deduplicate, and test for trigger behaviors. Model extraction queries an API at scale to train a copy, and model inversion or membership inference tries to recover training data; mitigate with rate limits, anomaly detection, output limits and avoiding sensitive training data. System prompt extraction is easy, so never put secrets in prompts and assume instructions will leak.",["Backdoors via poisoned data","Training data vetting","Extraction and inversion attacks","Rate limiting and anomaly detection","System prompt leakage"])],
[Q("An injected email makes the agent send the report to attacker@evil.com instead of the user's manager. This is…",["Model extraction","Tool parameter tampering","Data poisoning","Reward hacking"],1,"The goal stays the same, but a tool argument was manipulated."),
Q("Best defense against an MCP 'rug pull'?",["Higher temperature","Pin and diff tool manifests, re-approve on change","Bigger context window","Disable logging"],1,"Detecting changed tool definitions prevents silent behavior changes."),
Q("Why never put secrets in system prompts?",["They cost tokens","System prompts can be extracted by users","They slow inference","Models ignore them"],1,"Prompt extraction is routine; secrets belong in secret managers.")],
["policy-gate"])),
("trust","t-l3",S("t-responsible","Responsible AI: Fairness, Transparency and Content Safety",
"Responsible AI turns values like fairness and transparency into tests, controls and disclosures that ship with the product.",
"Responsible AI covers fairness, reliability and safety, privacy, inclusiveness, transparency and accountability (Microsoft's principles; similar lists from Google, NIST and the OECD). For engineers it becomes concrete work.\n\nFairness and bias: measure outcome differences across groups (demographic parity, equalized odds) for decisions such as screening or credit, and test generative outputs for stereotyped or uneven quality across languages, dialects and names. Content safety: classify and filter harmful inputs and outputs with moderation APIs (OpenAI Moderation, Azure AI Content Safety, Bedrock Guardrails, Llama Guard), tuned per use case and audience. Transparency: tell users they are talking to AI, explain limitations, and document the system with model cards, system cards or transparency notes. Human oversight: meaningful review for consequential decisions, with the ability to contest.\n\nThis aligns with regulation: the EU AI Act prohibits certain practices, requires transparency (Article 50) and imposes data governance and bias-examination duties on high-risk systems.\n\nCommon mistakes: one aggregate accuracy number hiding group disparities; safety filters so strict they block legitimate use; ethics as a one-time review instead of ongoing tests.\n\nMental model: if a value is not measured, it is not a requirement.",
[R("Microsoft Responsible AI Standard","https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/ai/principles-and-approach"),R("Fairlearn","https://fairlearn.org/"),R("Azure AI Content Safety","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/overview"),R("Model Cards for Model Reporting (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993")],
"For a resume-screening assistant, build a fairness eval with synthetic resumes varied only by name and gender cues, measure selection-rate differences, and mitigate. Add content safety filtering with a moderation API, tune thresholds on 100 labeled examples, and write a transparency note and model card.",
[T("t-responsible-t1","Fairness and bias testing","Define the decision and the groups that matter, then measure: selection rates per group (demographic parity), error rates per group (equalized odds), and calibration. For LLMs, use counterfactual tests that swap names, genders or dialects while holding qualifications fixed, and compare outputs. Report disaggregated metrics, not one average. Mitigations include better data, prompt and policy changes, post-processing thresholds and human review, each re-measured. Fairlearn and similar toolkits help compute and visualize disparities.",["Choosing groups and decisions","Demographic parity and equalized odds","Counterfactual prompt tests","Disaggregated reporting","Mitigation and re-measurement"]),
T("t-responsible-t2","Content safety and moderation","Content safety systems classify text and images into harm categories (hate, violence, sexual content, self-harm) with severity levels, and detect jailbreak or injection attempts. Use them on inputs, tool outputs and model outputs. Tune thresholds per audience: a children's tutor and a security research tool need different settings. Measure both misses and false blocks on labeled samples, give users a clear message when blocked, and log decisions for review and appeals.",["Harm categories and severity","Moderation APIs and Llama Guard","Thresholds per audience","False blocks vs misses","Block messages and appeals"]),
T("t-responsible-t3","Transparency, documentation and oversight","Tell people when they interact with AI and when content is AI-generated, as EU AI Act Article 50 requires in many cases. Document systems with model cards, system cards or transparency notes covering intended use, limitations, evaluation results and known risks. Provide meaningful human oversight for consequential decisions: reviewers need the evidence, time and authority to override, and affected people need a way to contest. Review documentation whenever models or prompts change.",["AI disclosure to users","Model cards and transparency notes","Limitations and intended use","Meaningful human oversight","Contestability and appeals"])],
[Q("Why report fairness metrics disaggregated by group?",["Saves compute","A single average can hide large disparities between groups","Required by Kafka","To raise accuracy"],1,"Aggregates mask subgroup harms."),
Q("A counterfactual fairness test for an LLM screener changes…",["The model weights","Only identity cues like names while keeping qualifications fixed","The tokenizer","Temperature"],1,"Holding qualifications constant isolates the effect of identity cues."),
Q("Content safety thresholds should be…",["Identical for every product","Tuned per audience and measured for misses and false blocks","Disabled in production","Set by the model itself"],1,"Different audiences need different trade-offs.")],
[]))]

EXTRA_TOPICS={
"i-gateway":T("i-gateway-x1","Kubernetes Gateway API Inference Extension and llm-d","The Kubernetes Gateway API Inference Extension adds model-aware routing to standard gateways: an InferencePool groups model-server pods, and an endpoint picker chooses a pod using live signals such as queue depth, KV-cache utilization and loaded LoRA adapters. llm-d builds on vLLM and this extension for distributed inference on Kubernetes, adding prefix-cache-aware routing, disaggregated prefill and decode, and KV cache offloading. Databricks AI Gateway and similar products provide the higher-level policy layer.",["InferencePool and endpoint picker","Queue- and KV-aware scheduling","llm-d architecture","KV cache offloading","Policy gateways (Databricks AI Gateway) vs inference routers"]),
"d-connect":T("d-connect-x1","Warehouse-native agents: Genie, Cortex Analyst and Unity Catalog","Data platforms now ship their own text-to-SQL agents. Databricks Genie answers questions over Unity Catalog tables using curated instructions, example queries and certified metrics; Snowflake Cortex Analyst uses semantic models, and Cortex Agents orchestrate search and SQL. They keep data and governance inside the platform, with Unity Catalog or Snowflake roles enforcing access. Treat them like any text-to-SQL agent: curate the semantic context, evaluate with golden questions and expose them to other agents as tools.",["Databricks Genie spaces","Snowflake Cortex Analyst and semantic models","Unity Catalog permissions","Golden questions for text-to-SQL agents","Exposing platform agents as tools"]),
"a-fde":T("a-fde-x1","Workshops, exec readouts and enterprise platform integration","FDEs run discovery workshops: map the workflow on a whiteboard with the people who do it, list systems and data, and agree on the metric. Exec readouts are short: problem, result versus baseline, risks, cost and next step. Most enterprise value runs through SAP, Salesforce, ServiceNow, Workday and Microsoft 365, so learn their APIs, event models, permission schemes and the vendor's own agent features before building custom connectors.",["Discovery workshop format","Exec readout structure","SAP, Salesforce, ServiceNow APIs","Vendor-native agent features","Change management with users"]),
"t-evalfound":T("t-evalfound-x1","Evaluation-driven development and model selection","Evaluation-driven development makes evals the main loop: write cases first, change prompts, tools or models, and keep a change only if the eval improves without regressions. The same suite drives model selection. Public benchmarks and leaderboards shortlist candidates, but contamination and task mismatch mean only your own golden set decides. Compare quality, latency and cost per correct answer for each candidate, and re-run the comparison when new models ship.",["Evals-first iteration loop","Model selection with your own golden set","Benchmark contamination","Cost per correct answer","Re-evaluating on new releases"]),
"e-rag":T("e-rag-x1","Knowledge graphs for RAG","Knowledge graphs store entities and typed relationships, such as customer owns contract and contract references a policy, which supports multi-hop questions that chunk similarity misses. Build them by extracting entities and relations with an LLM, resolving duplicates, and storing them in a graph database. At query time, combine vector search to find entry points with graph traversal or Cypher queries to follow relations. GraphRAG adds community summaries for global questions. Graphs cost effort to maintain, so use them where relationships drive answers.",["Entities and relations","LLM-based extraction and entity resolution","Graph databases and Cypher","Vector entry points plus traversal","When knowledge graphs pay off"]),
"t-cloudsec":T("t-cloudsec-x1","Securing managed AI services: Bedrock, Vertex AI and Azure AI Foundry","Each cloud provides AI-specific controls. On AWS, Bedrock offers private VPC endpoints, IAM condition keys per model, Bedrock Guardrails for content and PII filtering, and model invocation logging. On Google Cloud, Vertex AI supports VPC Service Controls, CMEK, workload identity and Model Armor for prompt and response screening. Azure AI Foundry uses private networking, managed identity, Entra-based access and Content Safety with Prompt Shields. Enable these by default in landing zones and audit them with CSPM.",["Bedrock IAM, endpoints, Guardrails, logging","Vertex AI VPC-SC, CMEK, Model Armor","Azure AI Foundry managed identity and Prompt Shields","Default-on landing zone controls","CSPM checks for AI services"]),
"t-agentops":T("t-agentops-x1","Tooling landscape: Langfuse, LangSmith, Phoenix and more","Agent observability tools share a model of traces, spans, sessions, scores and datasets. Langfuse is open source with tracing, prompt management and evals. LangSmith is tightly integrated with LangChain and LangGraph. Arize Phoenix is open source and OpenTelemetry-native. Braintrust focuses on evals and experiments, and Datadog and Sentry add LLM tracing to existing APM. Prefer tools that ingest OpenTelemetry GenAI spans so you can switch vendors without re-instrumenting, and check data residency for stored prompts.",["Langfuse","LangSmith","Arize Phoenix","Braintrust, Datadog, Sentry","OpenTelemetry for portability"]),
"e-post":T("e-post-x1","Post-training pipeline end to end","A typical post-training pipeline runs supervised fine-tuning on curated demonstrations, then preference optimization (DPO or RLHF with a reward model), then reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards for reasoning and tool use, often with GRPO. Each stage needs held-out evals for capability and safety regressions. Pretraining checkpoints set the ceiling; post-training shapes behavior. Track data provenance throughout, and compare every stage against the previous checkpoint and a prompting baseline.",["SFT on demonstrations","Preference optimization: DPO and RLHF","RL with verifiable rewards","Safety and capability regression evals","Data provenance across stages"]),
}
